<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="Clinical hydration principles">
  <div><strong>Assess the cause</strong><span>Low intake, illness, medications and organ function can require very different responses.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Choose the right route</strong><span>Oral intake is appropriate only when the person can drink safely and the clinical situation supports it.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Follow the authorized use</strong><span>Best Hydrate’s Natural Product Number does not authorize every proposed medical application.</span></div>
</div>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Clinical oral hydration</p>
    <h2>The same drink can mean different things in different patients</h2>
    <p>In healthcare, fluid decisions may depend on swallowing safety, ongoing losses, kidney or heart function, medications, glucose management and the reason a person is not drinking normally.</p>
    <p>A plausible hydration mechanism is not the same as a validated clinical indication.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Before recommending an oral product</p>
  <h2>Four questions matter</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4">
    <article><h3>Can the person swallow safely?</h3><p>Dysphagia or reduced consciousness can make ordinary liquids inappropriate.</p></article>
    <article><h3>What is being lost?</h3><p>Low intake, sweat, diarrhoea, vomiting and medication can create different fluid problems.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Are there restrictions?</h3><p>Heart, kidney, liver and endocrine conditions can change safe fluid or electrolyte targets.</p></article>
    <article><h3>What evidence applies?</h3><p>Claims should match the actual product, population, dose and outcome studied.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Best Hydrate and therapeutic ORS</p>
    <h2>Shared ingredients do not make two products equivalent</h2>
    <p>World Health Organization reduced-osmolarity oral rehydration salts are a defined therapeutic formulation for diarrhoeal dehydration. Best Hydrate should not be described as therapeutic ORS unless formulation, authorization and evidence support that exact claim.</p>
  </article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout">
    <span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Do not conflate</span>
    <h3>Consumer electrolyte drink ≠ therapeutic ORS.</h3>
    <p>Clinical equivalence requires more than overlapping ingredients.</p>
  </aside>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Where research can go</p>
  <h2>Questions worth testing properly</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--3">
    <article><h3>Adherence and acceptability</h3><p>Will the target population prepare and consume the product consistently?</p></article>
    <article><h3>Hydration outcomes</h3><p>Do relevant hydration measures improve against an appropriate comparator?</p></article>
    <article><h3>Implementation</h3><p>Can preparation, storage, staffing and procurement fit the care setting?</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

